</div></div>
<div class="panel"><h2>Activity scatter — float vs duration</h2>
<p class="muted">Source: <b>MLC3_IMS_2026-06.mpp</b>. One dot per activity: <b>total float</b> (x) against <b>duration</b> (y), red = critical (progress-aware), diamonds = milestones. Long-duration, low-float activities sit at the lower-left — the schedule's pressure points a count metric never reveals. The full activity grid above is the accessible data table.</p>
<p><b>What this data says:</b> of the <b>100</b> incomplete activities, <b>36</b> (36.0%) have zero-or-negative float (no room to slip), <b>16</b> more sit within 10 working days of it, and <b>29</b> (29.0%) carry more than 44 wd of float — float that high usually means missing logic (DCMA-06), not genuine slack. The median float is <b>9 wd</b>. The schedule's ability to absorb a slip rests on how the low-float band is managed; the table below names the biggest pressure points — the longest tasks with the least room.</p>
<details><summary class="btn-link">Top pressure points (longest low-float work)</summary><table><tbody><tr><th scope="col">UID</th><th scope="col">Activity</th><th scope="col">Duration (wd)</th><th scope="col">Float (wd)</th><th scope="col">%</th></tr>
<tr><td>2222</td><td>Vehicle — Integrated Vehicle Test (IVT)</td><td>8</td><td>0</td><td>0%</td></tr>
<tr><td>2156</td><td>LCM — Acoustic Blanket Install</td><td>5</td><td>-4</td><td>0%</td></tr>
<tr><td>2239</td><td>Vehicle — WDR Data Review &amp; Scrub Fixes</td><td>5</td><td>0</td><td>0%</td></tr>
<tr><td>2244</td><td>Vehicle — Final Vehicle Closeouts</td><td>5</td><td>0</td><td>0%</td></tr>
<tr><td>2247</td><td>Schedule Margin — Pad Ops</td><td>5</td><td>0</td><td>0%</td></tr>
</tbody></table></details>
<div class="chart-host" id="scatterChart" data-name="MLC-3 IMS 2026-06"></div></div>
<div class="panel"><h2>Total-float distribution</h2>
<p class="muted">Activities binned by <b>total float</b> (working days), in DCMA-aligned bands. Mass at <b>0 / &lt; 0</b> is the critical-and-behind core; a spike in the <b>&gt; 44 d</b> band is float padding or missing successor logic (DCMA-06). <b>Click a bar</b> to list that band's activities on the right; add columns with the selector and export the selection to Excel.</p>
<div class="hist-split">
<div class="chart-host hist-left" id="floatHist" data-name="MLC-3 IMS 2026-06"></div>
<div class="hist-right" id="floatHistDrill"><p class="muted">Click a histogram bar to see the activities in that float band here.</p></div>
</div></div>
<div class="panel"><h2>Working calendar</h2>
<p class="muted">The time basis behind every computed date, float, and day-denominated
threshold — imported from the file's project calendar (the standard 8h/Mon-Fri default
when the file carries none).</p>
<table><tbody>
<tr><th scope="col">Calendar</th><td>MLC Standard 5x8</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="col">Working day</th><td>8 h/day (480 min)</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="col">Work week</th><td>Monday, Tuesday, Wednesday, Thursday, Friday</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="col">Holidays</th><td>5 — 07/03/2026, 09/07/2026, 11/26/2026, 11/27/2026, 12/25/2026</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<div class="panel"><h2>Float analysis — low-float bands</h2>
<p class="muted">Of the 100 incomplete activities, how many are running out of room: at 0 days
of float (critical or negative), under 5, and under 10 working days — cumulative bands on
this schedule's calendar. A swelling low-float band is the early warning that the schedule is
losing its ability to absorb slips.</p>
<table><tbody><tr><th scope="col"></th><th scope="col">0 days</th><th scope="col">&lt; 5 days</th><th scope="col">&lt; 10 days</th></tr>
<tr><th scope="col" class="metric-th"><span class="dcma-metric mhelp" tabindex="0" role="button" title="Total float — the working days an activity can slip before it delays the project finish.">Total float <span class="dcma-info" aria-hidden="true">ⓘ</span></span></th><td>36 <span class="muted">(36%)</span></td><td>41 <span class="muted">(41%)</span></td><td>52 <span class="muted">(52%)</span></td></tr>
<tr><th scope="col" class="metric-th"><span class="dcma-metric mhelp" tabindex="0" role="button" title="Free float — the working days an activity can slip before it delays its immediate successor.">Free float <span class="dcma-info" aria-hidden="true">ⓘ</span></span></th><td>43 <span class="muted">(43%)</span></td><td>74 <span class="muted">(74%)</span></td><td>89 <span class="muted">(89%)</span></td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<div class="panel"><h2>Completion performance</h2>
<p class="muted">How the completed work actually performed against its baseline: the
ahead / on-schedule / behind split, the days gained and lost, and actual-vs-baseline
durations. Day variances are calendar days.</p>
<table><tbody>
<tr><th scope="col" class="metric-th">Completed ahead of baseline</th><td>16 of 150 (10.7%)</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="col" class="metric-th">Completed on schedule</th><td>98 of 150 (65.3%)</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="col" class="metric-th">Completed behind baseline</th><td>36 of 150 (24%)</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="col" class="metric-th">Average days ahead (early finishers)</th><td>2 days (over 16)</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="col" class="metric-th">Average days late (late finishers)</th><td>6.8 days (over 36)</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="col" class="metric-th">Average completion variance (+ = late)</th><td>1.4 days (over 150)</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="col" class="metric-th">Activities longer than planned</th><td>22 of 150 (14.7%)</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="col" class="metric-th">Activities shorter than baseline</th><td>9 of 150 (6%)</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="col" class="metric-th">Duration ratio (actual / baseline) — min</th><td>0.8</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="col" class="metric-th">Duration ratio — average</th><td>1.12</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="col" class="metric-th">Duration ratio — max</th><td>1.9</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="col" class="metric-th">MEI (milestones finished / milestones due)</th><td>1</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="col" class="metric-th">EPI (execution events recorded / events expected)</th><td>0.91</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="col" class="metric-th">Start-to-Finish Ratio (scheduled pairs / actual pairs)</th><td>1.04</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="col" class="metric-th">Schedule elapsed since latest actual finish</th><td>3 days (over 3)</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<div class="panel"><h2>Structural health checks</h2>
<p class="muted">Deterministic schedule-construction checks from the NASA Schedule Management Handbook (Fig. 6-9), beyond DCMA-14 — green = clear, otherwise the count and the first offending activities (the activity grid above is the full record).</p>
<div class="finding sev-MEDIUM"><div class="finding-head"><span class="rk-score rk-high">3</span> <b>Critical merge hotspots</b></div><p>Critical activities with more than two predecessors — a delay in any feeder hits the critical path; a fragile convergence point.</p><p class="cite">UID 2220, UID 2226, UID 9001</p></div>
<div class="finding sev-INFO"><div class="finding-head"><span class="rk-score rk-min">✓ clear</span> <b>Critical diverge hotspots</b></div><p>Critical activities driving more than two successors — a slip here fans out across many downstream paths.</p></div>
<div class="finding sev-INFO"><div class="finding-head"><span class="rk-score rk-min">✓ clear</span> <b>Level-of-effort on the critical path</b></div><p>Level-of-effort (support) tasks should never be on the critical path — the handbook says the critical path cannot include LOE; their presence signals broken logic.</p></div>
<div class="finding sev-INFO"><div class="finding-head"><span class="rk-score rk-min">✓ clear</span> <b>Milestones with duration</b></div><p>Milestones are zero-duration events; a milestone carrying duration is mis-typed and distorts counts and logic.</p></div>
<div class="finding sev-INFO"><div class="finding-head"><span class="rk-score rk-min">✓ clear</span> <b>Zero-duration tasks (non-milestone)</b></div><p>Non-milestone activities with zero duration are usually mis-typed milestones or placeholders and should be reviewed.</p></div>
<div class="finding sev-INFO"><div class="finding-head"><span class="rk-score rk-min">✓ clear</span> <b>Hidden duration (lag &gt; 35% of duration)</b></div><p>A predecessor lag longer than 35% of the activity's duration hides real work inside a relationship, where it escapes status and risk — model it as an activity instead.</p></div>
<div class="finding sev-MEDIUM"><div class="finding-head"><span class="rk-score rk-high">4</span> <b>Estimated (placeholder) durations</b></div><p>Activities whose duration is still flagged 'Estimated' in MS Project are placeholders the planner has not firmed up — an under-developed estimate that should be replaced with a basis-backed duration before the schedule is relied upon.</p><p class="cite">UID 2262, UID 2263, UID 2264, UID 2266</p></div>
<div class="finding sev-INFO"><div class="finding-head"><span class="rk-score rk-min">✓ clear</span> <b>Missing WBS</b></div><p>Activities with no WBS code drop out of WBS rollups and traceability — every activity should map to the work-breakdown structure.</p></div>
<div class="finding sev-INFO"><div class="finding-head"><span class="rk-score rk-min">✓ clear</span> <b>Missing baseline finish</b></div><p>Without a baseline finish an activity can't be measured for execution (BEI, variance) — the schedule is not fully baselined.</p></div>
</div>
<div class="panel"><h2>Logic integrity</h2>
<p class="muted">Forensic logic-construction checks from the NASA Schedule Management Handbook (Fig. 6-9), beyond DCMA-14 — <b>out-of-sequence</b> progress (work recorded in an order the logic forbids) and <b>redundant logic</b> (a direct link a longer path already implies). Green = clear, otherwise the count and the first offending links (written predecessor→successor by UniqueID).</p>
<div class="finding sev-MEDIUM"><div class="finding-head"><span class="rk-score rk-high">2</span> <b>Out-of-sequence logic (FS)</b><span class="muted"> of 186 link(s)</span></div><p>Finish-to-start successors that recorded progress before their predecessor finished (the successor's actual start precedes the predecessor's actual finish, or the predecessor has no recorded finish while the successor has already started) — work done in an order the logic forbids, a status-override / broken-logic signature.</p><p class="cite">2140→2141, 2172→2173</p></div>
<div class="finding sev-INFO"><div class="finding-head"><span class="rk-score rk-min">✓ clear</span> <b>Redundant logic links</b><span class="muted"> of 186 link(s)</span></div><p>Direct links made superfluous because a longer path between the same two activities already exists, so the link constrains nothing the network did not already imply. Redundant logic clutters the model and masks the true driving path (Handbook Fig. 6-9).</p></div>
</div>
<div class="panel"><h2>Constraint health</h2>
<p class="muted">How imposed dates fare against the network logic (NASA Schedule Management Handbook, Fig. 6-9): a <b>hard constraint</b> the CPM date runs past cannot be honored, and a <b>deadline</b> the logic finish overruns is artificial negative float. Green = clear, otherwise the count and the first offending activities.</p>
<div class="finding sev-INFO"><div class="finding-head"><span class="rk-score rk-min">✓ clear</span> <b>Unsatisfied date constraints</b><span class="muted"> of 1</span></div><p>Hard-constrained activities whose imposed no-later-than / must date the logic already runs past (the CPM early date exceeds the constraint date) — the date cannot be honored without negative float. Resolve the logic or relax the date.</p></div>
<div class="finding sev-MEDIUM"><div class="finding-head"><span class="rk-score rk-high">1</span> <b>Deadlines breached (negative float)</b><span class="muted"> of 1</span></div><p>Activities whose logic finish runs past a set deadline (early finish &gt; deadline) — the artificial negative float a deadline imposes, signalling the plan can't meet a committed date on the current logic.</p><p class="cite">UID 1107</p></div>
</div>
<div class="panel"><h2>Vertical integration</h2>
<p class="muted">Whether each summary (rollup) bar envelopes the detail activities beneath it (by WBS nesting), using the schedule's stored dates — the handbook's vertical-traceability check. A parent that starts after its earliest child or finishes before its latest is an inconsistent rollup.</p>
<div class="finding sev-INFO"><div class="finding-head"><span class="rk-score rk-min">✓ clear</span> <b>Inconsistent vertical integration</b><span class="muted"> of 38 summary group(s)</span></div><p>Every summary's stored span envelopes the stored dates of the work beneath it.</p></div>
</div>
<div class="panel"><h2>Schedule variance (time)</h2>
<p class="muted">The NASA Schedule Management Handbook (§7.3.3.1) time view of progress. <b>SVt = ES − AT</b> (Earned Schedule minus Actual Time): positive is <b>ahead</b> of plan (favorable), negative is <b>behind</b> (unfavorable) — the count-based Earned-Schedule companion to SPI(t). Per-activity <b>finish</b> variance is a completed activity's actual finish minus its baseline finish; <b>start</b> variance is a started activity's actual start minus its baseline start (in working days, positive = late) — the latter surfaces in-progress slippage before tasks complete.</p>
<div class="stat-grid">
<div class="stat-card"><div class="stat-value">-9.4 wd (behind)</div><div class="stat-label">Schedule variance (SVt = ES - AT)</div></div>
<div class="stat-card"><div class="stat-value">98.6 wd</div><div class="stat-label">Earned Schedule (ES)</div></div>
<div class="stat-card"><div class="stat-value">108 wd</div><div class="stat-label">Actual Time (AT)</div></div>
<div class="stat-card"><div class="stat-value">150</div><div class="stat-label">Completed (finish variance)</div></div>
<div class="stat-card"><div class="stat-value">+1.4 wd</div><div class="stat-label">Mean finish variance</div></div>
<div class="stat-card"><div class="stat-value">156</div><div class="stat-label">Started (start variance)</div></div>
<div class="stat-card"><div class="stat-value">+1.1 wd</div><div class="stat-label">Mean start variance</div></div>
</div>
<h3>Largest finish variances (actual − baseline)</h3>
